[THOUGHT] CBC is normal — no eosinophilia, no leukocytosis, no anemia or thrombocytopenia. This makes systemic allergic reaction, parasitic infection (e.g., strongyloides), or hematologic malignancy unlikely. Combined with negative scabies scraping, absence of scaling/vesicles/crusting on exam, and classic linear/row-like papules in a traveler, the diagnosis strongly converges on **insect bite reaction**, most consistent with **bedbug bites**. All eight domains are now addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: pruritic red bumps on right thigh (recent onset);  
2) Course: appeared after Miami conference, linear/row-like progression;  
3) Associated symptoms: itching on multiple sites, no systemic symptoms;  
4) PMH: diabetes, HTN, hyperlipidemia — no skin history;  
5) Meds: enalapril, metformin, atorvastatin — no allergies;  
6) Exposure: recent conference travel (high-risk for bedbugs);  
7) Physical exam: linear papules, rows of bumps, excoriations;  
8) Testing: negative scabies scrape, normal CBC, no fungal workup needed given morphology.  
No critical gaps remain.

[FINISH]

[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. Although the patient did not report a central punctum or blood spot — which would strongly support insect bite — the absence of this finding does not rule it out (puncta may be subtle or obscured by scratching). Critically, all eight evidence domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: pruritic red bumps on right thigh, recent onset;  
2) Course: appeared post-Miami conference, linear/row-like progression;  
3) Associated symptoms: itching, excoriations, no systemic features;  
4) PMH: diabetes, HTN, hyperlipidemia;  
5) Meds/allergies: enalapril/metformin/atorvastatin, no allergies;  
6) Exposure: recent conference travel (bedbug risk);  
7) Physical exam: linear papules, rows, excoriations;  
8) Testing: negative scabies scrape, normal CBC, attempted dermoscopy (unavailable), and targeted query for punctum (negative but non-definitive).  

No further turns remain. The preponderance of evidence — especially linear/row-like distribution, excoriations, travel history, and exclusion of scabies — makes **insect bite reaction (most likely bedbug bites)** the single most likely diagnosis.

[FINISH]

